;; Gate thresholds and budgets, as data rather than constants in code.
;;
;; This is the AHE component-observability idea applied cheaply: every
;; threshold is a file a run records the digest of, so a later evolution loop
;; has somewhere to write and every pass-rate change localizes to one file.
;;
;; Each budget declares its KIND, which is the distinction dirge PR 739 found
;; made the thresholds legible:
;;
;;   :cost-ceiling  bounds sub-LLM spend. Never scaled up for a struggling run
;;                  — that means spending more on the run already in trouble.
;;   :re-fire-guard compensates for a predicate that cannot tell "happened"
;;                  from "happened and I already reacted". Safe to scale.
;;   :threshold     a real decision boundary.
;;
;; `capability-tunable?` is false wherever the capability tier has no standing
;; to move the number. The tier observes tool-call mechanics only, so it may
;; tune argument-hygiene budgets and may never relax a verification or
;; progress guard (dirge PR 740).

{:cull-threshold
 {:value 3 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Consecutive failed verifications before a branch is culled."}

 :cull-recent-window
 {:value 5 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "A confirmed artifact this recently protects a branch from culling."}

 :stuck-threshold
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Consecutive unproductive verifications before the stuck gate fires.

        STRICTLY BELOW cull-threshold, and that is the whole point (vf-31m).
        Both were 3, so the gate that says change your approach arrived on the
        turn the branch became eligible to be killed for not having changed
        it — and every move it predicts (retract, decompose, change technique)
        costs at least one turn. The README recorded the consequence without
        naming the cause: stuck fired and was obeyed zero times. A gate whose
        advice takes turns to follow, firing at the moment turns run out, is
        broken on inspection.

        gate-config-is-coherent asserts the inequality, so a later tuning pass
        cannot quietly close the gap again."}

 :reframe-grace
 {:value 4 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch is spared the consecutive-failure cull after the stuck
        gate has dropped it into a reframe, and the window the withheld
        approach stays withheld for.

        A branch told to abandon its approach carries the failures that caused
        the reframe, so without this it is culled mid-reframe for exactly the
        thing it was told to stop doing (vf-31m). The reprieve machinery
        already existed for Pareto-spared branches and is documented there as
        'a loan with a clock, not an exemption'; this is the same shape.

        The loan and the restriction expire together, deliberately: it would
        be incoherent to keep refusing an approach after the branch has lost
        the protection it was given to replace it. cull-hard-multiple still
        ends it early — a branch failing at twice the cull threshold is not
        reframing."}

 :progress-stall-threshold
 {:value 4 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns with no progress event before the stall nudge fires. Arms only
        after the first progress event; see prologue-cap for the other case."}

 :prologue-cap
 {:value 8 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch may take having produced nothing at all. The stall
        counter arms on a progress event, so a run that produced nothing never
        armed it and the monitor was structurally blind to the one case it most
        needed to report (dirge PR 739, dirge-t5dh)."}

 :max-stall-nudges
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the stall nudge may re-fire on one branch."}

 :studying-turns
 {:value 10 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Consecutive inspection-only turns (read/grep/eval/…), after the branch
        has shipped at least once, before the supervisor's studying gate fires.
        High enough that opening exploration is never nagged; a live run
        over-studied for twenty (karamazov-j5t)."}

 :max-studying-nudges
 {:value 3 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the studying nudge may re-fire on one branch."}

 :max-stuck-hints
 {:value 3 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the stuck hint may re-fire on one branch."}

 :max-milestone-nudges
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The milestone fires once per branch, on the first confirmation."}

 :max-emergency-reviews
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The emergency review fires once per branch. It was the one steer gate
        with no guard, and a live knights-3 run (2026-08-06) showed why that
        matters: its precondition held across three consecutive boundaries and
        it re-fired on every one, all predictions unmet — the exact
        happened-versus-already-reacted blindness re-fire guards exist for.
        Once: the steer either lands or the cull proceeds on its own."}

 :max-done-blocks
 {:value 3 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often `done` may be refused before the branch is told to give up.
        A cost ceiling because each refusal spends another model turn."}

 :turn-budget-notices
 {:value [0.6 0.85] :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Fractions of the turn cap at which the model is told where it stands.
        The cap was enforced but invisible to the model (dirge PR 738)."}

 :wind-down-fraction
 {:value 0.85 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Fraction of the turn cap at which the branch is told to stop exploring
        and ship what it has. Sized so the reserved tail fits an audit plus a
        done call for a branch with confirmed artifacts: UCLA's harness found a
        run with no reserved shipping tail spends its whole budget exploring
        and ships nothing."}

 :max-wind-down-steers
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the wind-down rung may fire on one branch. Once: the steer
        either lands or it does not."}

 :last-call-window
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How many of a branch's final turns the last-call rung fires in. The
        wind-down rung asks a branch to ship and is a soft nudge a model can
        ignore — a live team run (karamazov-pab) had every worker burn its whole
        cap without one done or give_up. This rung fires in the last turns and
        FORCES a done attempt (a done-tool prefill, not prose), so a run that
        will not converge at least ships its best partial rather than exhausting
        silently. Small: it is the last resort, not a second wind-down."}

 :max-last-call-steers
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the last-call rung may fire on one branch. Matched to the
        window so it can force a done on each of the final turns."}

 :max-safe-state-aborts
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the safe-state rung may fire on one branch. Not
        capability-tunable: this rung spends a hard-capped abort and, in a
        non-advisory mode, rebuilds an engine process."}

 :max-total-branches
 {:value 8 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Total branches per run including forked children. A cost ceiling:
        every branch is a concurrent provider call per turn plus its own
        engine process.

        Lowered from 15 after gen-21 measured what 15 actually costs. Asked
        for width 5, it reached 11 active by round 18 — beam_width is the
        floor the harness refills toward, not a cap — and spent 2.46M tokens
        for 4 confirmed artifacts before being stopped. The forks were
        genuinely distinct, so this is not a diversity problem; it is that
        the ceiling was set without anyone having measured the bill."}

 :critic-every
 {:value 5 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns between critic scorings of one branch. Every scoring is a
        sub-LLM call, so this is spend, and it is per active branch."}

 :cull-hard-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold at which a Pareto-spared branch is culled
        regardless of its scores. The reprieve is a loan with a clock, not an
        exemption — Pareto's known weakness is permissiveness."}

 :fork-invite-floor
 {:value {:viability 4 :progress 3} :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Minimum critic scores before a branch is invited to fork. Below
        this, growth spends budget on lines not yet earning it."}

 :juvenile-grace
 {:value 8 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a newly forked branch may take before the cull rule applies to
        it at all. Generation 9 forked for the first time and every child was
        dead within twelve turns: progress and momentum are age-correlated,
        so a branch born at turn 15 scores 1 on progress by definition and its
        mature parent dominates it on every objective one turn later.
        Selection that runs before an offspring can express itself culls on
        age rather than on merit. Sized to fit a thesis, a first encoding and
        a first verification — long enough to produce one confirmation, short
        enough that a genuinely dead line does not linger."}

 :branch-out-cooldown
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Turns before the branch-out rung may ask the same branch again.
        Generation 9 fired it on turns 14, 15 and 16 — one fork, then two
        re-fires while the branch was still acting on the first — and the
        whole fork budget was gone by turn 16 of a 65-turn run. The
        precondition (a confirmation exists, the beam has room) stays true
        for as long as the branch is productive, which is exactly the
        happened-versus-already-reacted blindness a cooldown fixes."}

 :max-branch-outs
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the branch-out rung may fire on one branch. This is the
        reproduction half of the loop, so it is a guard rather than a cost
        ceiling: each firing asks for offspring, and a branch that keeps
        confirming should keep being asked, but not on every boundary while
        it is busy complying."}

 :max-repopulates
 {:value 8 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the repopulate rung may ask one branch to refill the beam.
        Higher than :max-branch-outs, because this fires when the beam is
        already below width and a run that stops asking at that point simply
        finishes at width one — which is what five straight campaign runs did.
        The per-branch fork-invite cooldown still spaces the asks out, so this
        is the ceiling on a long collapse, not on a burst."}

 :fork-invite-cooldown
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Turns before the same branch may be invited to fork again. The
        invitation predicate cannot tell 'strong' from 'strong and already
        asked', which is the shape re-fire guards exist for."}

 :explore-cap
 {:value 5 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch may spend in the explore prologue before it is forced
        into build. The sketch tool is new — no generation has run with it —
        so 5 is a judgement call with no data behind it: a couple of searches
        and a sketch attempt or two, against prologue-cap's 8. The forced
        transition is the release valve that keeps a branch which cannot get
        a skeleton to elaborate from being locked out of verification for the
        whole run."}

 :cull-mechanics-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold at which consecutive mechanics failures
        (malformed tool calls and harness refusals) cull a branch even with
        survivors to spare. Three bad fences is a model having a bad turn;
        twice that is a branch that cannot work the protocol."}

 :safe-state-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold in consecutive failures before the
        safe-state rung may fire. Deliberately harder to trigger than a
        cull: this rung spends a hard-capped abort and, in a non-advisory
        mode, rebuilds an engine process."}

 :max-branch-theses
 {:value 4 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Most theses one branch_theses call may propose. A cost ceiling:
        every proposal beyond the first becomes a sibling branch — a
        concurrent provider call per turn plus its own process."}

 :reflection-cadence
 {:value 15 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns between reflection nudges. Not capability-tunable: the nudge
        invites the branch to reshape its own loop (reload_cells), and the
        tier under observation should not loosen the cadence at which it is
        invited to mutate the machinery observing it."}

 :share
 {:value {:statuses #{:confirmed} :require-relevance? true}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The shared-pool entry condition (drg-4026 #5): which claim statuses
        an artifact must carry to export to sibling branches, and whether the
        relevance filter (advances-thesis?) additionally gates it. Engine-
        confirmed is the entry condition that separates this from
        self-reported results; relevance is the second one — an artifact
        that engages neither the branch's thesis nor the problem cost its
        own branch a turn, exported it becomes every branch's context
        (vf-8fl). require-relevance? off admits any confirmed artifact."}

 :critic-objectives
 {:value [:progress :momentum :distinctness :viability] :kind :threshold
  :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The critic's scoring rubric — all maximize, 1..5. :progress is
        engine-confirmed work accumulated over the branch's life,
        :momentum is whether the recent turns are productive, :distinctness
        is difference from the sibling theses (the beam's diversity lives
        here), :viability is whether the line has anywhere left to go.
        Retention reads all but :progress (critic.clj
        survival-objectives); :fork-invite-floor keys on these same names.
        The score prompt still names these four in prose until it moves to
        prompts/ (tier 2a) — until then this list tunes what parse-scores
        ACCEPTS, not what the prompt asks for."}

 :decompose-max-depth
 {:value 3 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How deep the decompose recursion may go before a stuck unit is a
        hard failure rather than split again. A cost ceiling: each level
        multiplies sub-agents, and a unit that still won't pass its tests
        three levels down is not a size problem."}

 :decompose-max-parts
 {:value 4 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Most sub-units the architect may split one unit into."}

 :judge-rules
 ;; drg-4026 #55: the judge's deterministic finalization rules — what counts
 ;; as a test run, a code edit, an outside claim — as data. A fixed
 ;; test-runner ecosystem is project policy, not kernel code: a Python or
 ;; Rust project retunes :test-run-regex and :verifier-message without
 ;; touching src. Regexes are stored as strings (EDN has no regex literal);
 ;; re-pattern at read.
 {:value
  {:test-run-regex
   "(?i)-M:test|-A:test|run-tests|\\bjolt\\s+test\\b|\\bpytest\\b|\\bcargo\\s+test\\b|\\bnpm\\s+test\\b"
   :code-ext-regex "(?i)\\.(clj|cljc|cljs|edn|py|ts|js|rs|go)$"
   :claim-regex "(?i)\\b(tested|all tests pass|passing|verified|ran the (tests|suite))\\b"
   :outside-claim-regex "(?i)(https?://|www\\.|according to|the docs say|the spec says|the api returns)"
   ;; #56: matched against the REGISTERED tool names (and the run's rows).
   ;; fetch_artifact / fetch_turn contain no web-family words, so they never
   ;; count as reaching out; a project that registers a web tool disables
   ;; the block by existing — its runs COULD have checked the claim.
   :outside-reach-regex "(?i)(web|browse|curl|wget|http)"
   :verifier-message
   "You changed code but never ran a test. Run the suite (`jolt -M:test`) or the focused test, confirm it passes, then finish."
   :claim-message
   "Your answer claims the work was tested or verified, but the run shows no test was run. Run it and confirm, or drop the claim."
   :outside-message
   "Your answer attributes a claim to something outside the repo (a URL or a secondary source). samizdat's registered tools work on the local repo; nothing in this run could have checked that source. Cite evidence from the repo, or from your own verified work in it."}
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "Deterministic finalization rules for the judge's free blocks. The
         detection engine lives in judge.clj; the vocabulary it matches on
         lives here, so it retunes per project at runtime."}

  :focused-verify
  ;; drg-4026 #47/48: the focused-verify conventions — which changed paths
  ;; count as test files, how a test path maps to its namespace, and the
  ;; command that runs just those namespaces. Project conventions, not
  ;; kernel code: a pytest or maven project retunes these without a
  ;; rebuild. The derivations live in verify.clj and read these at fire
  ;; time. :cmd-prefix is concatenated with the derived expression and a
  ;; closing single quote for sh -c; the expression's namespaces come
  ;; through :ns-whitelist-regex, so the quoted payload genuinely contains
  ;; no single quotes (review3 #1).
  {:value
   {:test-file-regex "(?i)(^|/)(test|spec)s?/|[_-](test|spec)\\.[a-z]+$"
    :root-strip-regex "^(test|gui|src)/"
    :ext-strip-regex "\\.cljc?$"
    :ns-whitelist-regex "[a-zA-Z0-9.*-]+"
    :cmd-prefix "jolt -A:test -e '"}
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "The focused-verify conventions as data (see the comment above)."}

  :ship-gates
  ;; drg-4026 #44: the model-free ship rungs as data, compiled at load by
  ;; ship.clj into predicate/message closures fired on the computed
  ;; evidence (answer, problem, evidence, uncovered-numbers — the symbols
  ;; the forms below reference). Vector order is the cond order it
  ;; replaces. The number-coverage rung guards against a fabricated
  ;; verification report but only fires when the run produced evidence to
  ;; check against: a coding run's proof is its test run, not confirmed
  ;; claims, so empty evidence must not refuse every figure (the first
  ;; self-modification run did the work correctly and could not ship it).
  {:value
   [{:name :answer-exists
     :when (str/blank? (str answer))
     :message "Supply an `answer` to ship."}
    {:name :figure-coverage
     :when (and (seq evidence) (seq uncovered-numbers))
     :message-form (str "Your answer states figures no artifact supports: "
                        (str/join ", " (map (fn [x] (str "`" x "`"))
                                            (take 8 uncovered-numbers)))
                        ".\nA number in an answer has to come from something"
                        " confirmed or measured — that is the difference between a"
                        " report and a fabricated one. Either verify these or"
                        " remove them from the answer.")}
    {:name :engages-problem
     :when (and (not (str/blank? (str problem)))
                (not (engages-problem? problem answer)))
     :message-form (str "This answer shares no substantive term with the problem"
                        " statement. Whatever it establishes, it is not an answer to"
                        " the question that was asked.")}]
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "The lexical ship rungs (drg-4026 #44) — adding a rung is a data
         edit; the evidence computation stays in ship.clj."}

  :tool-vocab
  ;; Tier 1a: the tool vocabularies the gates and their settlers read — data
 ;; here, alongside the thresholds, so a vocabulary changes at runtime
 ;; without a rebuild. agent-test walks every name against the registered
 ;; run-tools (review3 #6): a name the loop cannot dispatch is a gate whose
 ;; prediction silently narrows to :unmet.
 {:verification #{"eval" "shell"}
  :shipping #{"write_file" "edit_file" "shell" "reload_cells" "manifest"
              "done" "give_up" "thesis" "branch_theses"}
  :file-write #{"write_file" "edit_file"}
  :settle-called
  {:milestone        #{"done"}
   :branch-out       #{"branch_theses"}
   :repopulate       #{"branch_theses"}
   :emergency-review #{"done" "thesis"}
   :stuck            #{"thesis"}
   :safe-state       #{"thesis" "done" "give_up"}
   :done-blocked     #{"done" "give_up"}
   :turn-budget      #{"done" "give_up"}
   :wind-down        #{"done"}
   :last-call        #{"done" "give_up"}
   :reflection       #{"introspect" "reload_cells" "cells"}
   :studying         #{"write_file" "edit_file" "shell" "reload_cells"
                       "done" "give_up"}}}

 :gates
 ;; Tier 3a: the steer policy as data — pilot gates first. A :gates entry
 ;; carries :when as an EDN FORM, compiled by gates.clj at load into the
 ;; closure shape the arbiter reads (the manifest :dispatches are the
 ;; precedent: EDN predicates evaluated at compile time). The form sees the
 ;; gate context keys (directive done-block branch max-turns branch-count
 ;; safe-state-coverage) and calls the same accessors the hand-written
 ;; closures did — (threshold k) reads the config atom at fire time, so
 ;; tuning a threshold stays runtime-editable; only the form structure
 ;; compiles at load. :message-file names a prompts/ file; {{turn-count}}
 ;; and {{max-turns}} interpolate at fire time.
 [{:gate :reflection
   :priority 13
   :budget nil
   :doc "A quiet-turn nudge to reflect on the loop itself. Lowest priority, so
         it is passed over whenever any real steer holds and surfaces only on a
         turn where nothing else does. The loop is a mycelium workflow the
         branch can see (introspect) and reshape (reload_cells); a run that
         never reconsiders its own machinery keeps whatever friction it started
         with. Fires on a cadence, not a condition, which is why it sits below
         every gate that fires because something is wrong."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (pos? (state/turn-count branch))
              (zero? (mod (state/turn-count branch)
                          (threshold :reflection-cadence))))
   :message-file "reflection"
   :prediction "the branch inspects or reshapes its loop"
   :window 1}

  {:gate :prologue-cap
   :priority 9
   :budget nil
   :doc "The branch has produced nothing at all. Every other guard is
         principled-blind here: the stall counter arms on a progress event, the
         stuck gate needs failures, and a branch making successful, varied,
         useless calls trips neither.

         Build phase only (vf-9wx). A banked sketch is deliberately :neutral
         with progress? false — a plan is not progress — so a branch dropped
         back into :explore by a reframe accrues nothing while it re-plans, and
         without this guard is told at turn 41 that it is 41 turns in with
         nothing verified. True, and useless to a branch doing exactly what the
         harness just asked of it."
   :when (and (= :build (:phase branch))
              (not (:any-progress? branch))
              (>= (state/turn-count branch) (threshold :prologue-cap)))
   :message-file "prologue-cap"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are {{turn-count}} turns in with nothing verified."
   :prediction "the branch produces its first artifact"
   :window 3}

  ;; Tier 3b tranche 1: the seven gates whose messages are a prompts/ file
  ;; (plus {{turn-count}}/{{max-turns}} interpolation) — no message-builder
  ;; machinery needed. The five that build messages from branch state
  ;; (done-blocked, human-directive, safe-state, stuck, studying,
  ;; progress-stalled) remain closures until :message compiles as a form.

  ;; Priority 2.5: below safe-state (a branch just told it cannot ship is
  ;; not forced to ship) and above wind-down, so in the final turns the hard
  ;; force wins over the soft nudge. Fractional to slot between the two
  ;; without renumbering the ladder; priorities need only be distinct
  ;; (gate-config-is-coherent).
  {:gate :last-call
   :priority 2.5
   :budget :max-last-call-steers
   :tool "done"
   :doc "The branch is in its last turns and still has not shipped. wind-down
         already asked it to; this FORCES the attempt — a done-tool prefill, so
         the model must continue inside a done call rather than exploring or
         rambling for its final turns. A live team run (karamazov-pab) had every
         worker exhaust its cap with no done or give_up; a forced partial that
         the reviewer/critic can judge beats a silent exhaustion the supervisor
         has to re-task blind. If the branch truly has nothing, the done-gate
         refuses it and the done-blocked rung takes over toward giving up."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              max-turns
              (>= (state/turn-count branch)
                  (- max-turns (threshold :last-call-window))))
   :message-file "last-call"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are at turn {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}} — your last turns. Ship your best partial now with done."
   :prediction "the branch ships"
   :window 2}

  {:gate :wind-down
   :priority 3
   :budget :max-wind-down-steers
   :doc "The branch has passed the fraction of the turn cap reserved for
         shipping. UCLA's harness reserves a deadline tail for assembly and
         shipping, and a run with no such tail spends its whole budget
         exploring and ships nothing. This steer outranks every nudge rung so
         a branch near its cap is told to ship before it is nagged to do more,
         but it never outranks done-blocked or safe-state: a branch that has
         just been told it cannot ship is not being asked to ship."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (>= (state/turn-count branch)
                  (* (threshold :wind-down-fraction) (max 1 max-turns))))
   :message-file "wind-down"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are at turn {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}}."
   :prediction "the branch ships"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :emergency-review
   :priority 4
   :budget :max-emergency-reviews
   :doc "At the cull threshold but holding a recent confirmation. Rather than
         culling the branch that produced the most, tell it to ship what it
         has or change approach. Guarded since a live run re-fired it on
         three consecutive boundaries: the precondition persists while the
         branch is busy complying, which is what re-fire guards are for."
   :when (and (>= (:consecutive-failures branch) (threshold :cull-threshold))
              (state/confirmed-in-last branch (threshold :cull-recent-window)))
   :message-file "emergency-review"
   :prediction "the branch ships or changes technique"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :milestone
   :priority 5
   :budget :max-milestone-nudges
   :doc "First confirmed artifact on this branch that engages what the branch
         is working on. Runs that do not ship at this moment usually fail:
         after a confirmation the instinct is to push for more, and that
         usually loses the verified result. Relevance-filtered because the
         gate congratulated a branch for verifying that between/3 works
         (vf-8fl), which is the same failure the zebra run produced."
   :when (state/has-relevant-confirmed? branch)
   :message-file "milestone"
   :prediction "the branch ships within two turns"
   :window 2}

  ;; Ranked just above branch-out. Both ask for offspring, but this one
  ;; fires because the beam is dying rather than because a branch is
  ;; thriving, and refilling an empty slot is the more urgent of the two.
  ;; The scheduler marks the branch (it is the only thing that knows the
  ;; alive count, the target, and who is strongest); this rung is what
  ;; actually asks.
  {:gate :repopulate
   :priority 6
   :budget :max-repopulates
   :tool "branch_theses"
   :doc "The beam has fallen below its target width and this branch is the
         strongest survivor. It was an invitation appended straight from the
         scheduler until it became this: that carried no prediction, settled
         nothing, and showed up in no gate tally (gen-17 sent 12 in its
         first 120 turns and 9 were declined), and was a second harness
         voice on a boundary that had already had its one steer."
   :when (when-let [due (:repopulate-due branch)]
           ;; Only for a mark the branch has not already been asked about,
           ;; so a mark that survives on the branch does not re-fire every
           ;; boundary while it is busy complying.
           (let [last-fired (->> (:gate-history branch)
                                 (filter #(= :repopulate (:gate %)))
                                 (map :turn)
                                 (reduce max -1000))]
             (> due last-fired)))
   :message-file "repopulate"
   :prediction "the branch calls branch_theses"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :branch-out
   :priority 7
   :budget :max-branch-outs
   :tool "branch_theses"
   :doc "A branch that just confirmed something, with room left in the beam.

         This is the reproduction half of the loop. The beam had selection
         (critic scores, Pareto retention) and no variation: branch_theses
         existed, the scheduler invited forks, and across every live run
         the count of forked children was zero — seven invitations went out
         in one run and every one was declined. An invitation the model may
         decline is not a mechanism; a gate with a settled prediction is.

         Fires on evidence rather than on hope: a confirmation is the
         fitness signal, so the branch worth reproducing from is the one
         that just proved something ABOUT THE PROBLEM — a fork costs another
         engine process and another model call per turn, and is not worth
         spending on a branch that confirmed its own tooling. Silent once the
         run is winding down, because a new line that late cannot finish."
   :when (let [last-fired (->> (:gate-history branch)
                               (filter #(= :branch-out (:gate %)))
                               (map :turn)
                               (reduce max -1000))]
           ;; Not while the branch is still acting on the last ask.
           (and (state/has-relevant-confirmed? branch)
                (< (or branch-count 0) (threshold :max-total-branches))
                (>= (- (state/turn-count branch) last-fired)
                    (threshold :branch-out-cooldown))
                (< (state/turn-count branch)
                   (* (threshold :wind-down-fraction)
                      (max 1 (or max-turns 40))))))
   :message-file "branch-out"
   :prediction "the branch calls branch_theses"
   :window 3}

  ;; Reads the branch's own record of which fractions it has been told
  ;; about. Taking it from the context instead meant the caller had to
  ;; remember to thread it back, and on the first live run it did not — the
  ;; gate re-fired on every turn past 60%.
  {:gate :turn-budget
   :priority 12
   :budget nil
   ;; drg-4026 #4: the effect this gate has on branch state, applied by
   ;; loop/apply-effects — the fraction notices it has delivered are folded
   ;; into the branch so the gate stops re-firing (the comment above the
   ;; entry is the story of forgetting to do exactly that).
   :effect :notified-fractions
   :doc "The turn cap was enforced but invisible to the model, so it could not
         budget against it (dirge PR 738)."
   :when (let [used (/ (double (state/turn-count branch)) (max 1 max-turns))
               notified (:notified-fractions branch #{})]
           (boolean (some #(and (>= used %) (not (contains? notified %)))
                          (threshold :turn-budget-notices))))
   :message-suffix "**Turn budget**: you have used {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}} turns. Land what you can verify rather than opening a new line of attack."
   :prediction "the branch converges rather than starting something new"
   :window 3}

  ;; Tier 3b tranche 2: the six message-builder gates. Their :message-form is
  ;; an EDN form compiled like :when — same gate-context keys, plus
  ;; prompt/threshold/state — evaluated at fire time.

  {:gate :human-directive
   :priority 0
   :budget nil
   :doc "A human told the branch to do something. Outranks every machine gate,
         which is dirge PR 717's finding arriving as a design property rather
         than a bug fix."
   :when (some? directive)
   :message-form (str "**A human has intervened in this run.**\n\n"
                      (:payload directive)
                      "\n\nThis takes precedence over anything the harness has told"
                      " you. Act on it on this turn.")
   :prediction "the branch acts on the directive on the next turn"
   :window 1}

  {:gate :done-blocked
   :priority 1
   :budget :max-done-blocks
   :doc "done was called without the evidence it requires. The message says
         which gate is unmet, because 'not yet' with no reason produces
         another identical attempt."
   :when (some? done-block)
   :message-form done-block
   :prediction "the branch supplies the missing evidence or gives up"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :safe-state
   :priority 2
   :budget :max-safe-state-aborts
   :doc "DS1's third failure rung. The branch has failed repeatedly since a
         green ship-verify, so what it changed since is the suspect, not
         the approach. Advisory by default: it names the fallback rather
         than performing it, because a restore that is not fully covered
         produces a session that never existed."
   :when (state/safe-state-due? branch (threshold :cull-threshold)
                               (threshold :safe-state-multiple))
   :message-form (str (prompt "safe-state")
                      "\n\nYour last green test run was at turn "
                      (:green-snapshot branch) ". "
                      (if (:ok safe-state-coverage)
                        (str "Every turn since is journalled, so replaying up to"
                             " that point is a state the branch actually occupied"
                             " — you can retrace it by hand turn by turn.")
                        (str "The turn log no longer reaches that point: "
                             (:reason safe-state-coverage)
                             " Start from what the journal still shows, or ship"
                             " what you have.")))
   :prediction "the branch changes technique, or ships what it has"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :stuck
   :priority 8
   :budget :max-stuck-hints
   ;; drg-4026 #4: the effect this gate has on branch state, applied by
   ;; loop/apply-effects — the reframe that withholds the failing claim.
   :effect :begin-reframe
   :doc "Consecutive failed or repetitive verifications. Keyed on failure,
         which is why the progress gate below exists as well.

         The one gate that changes branch state rather than only speaking.
         It fired at cull-threshold originally, so the advice to change
         course arrived on the turn the branch became killable for not
         having changed it (vf-31m); stuck-threshold is now strictly lower
         and the firing opens a grace window. And it WITHHOLDS (vf-49o):
         the loop answers a firing by calling state/begin-reframe, after
         which re-verifying the failing approach is refused on every engine
         until the branch puts a different one on record. A gate is data
         and cannot mutate the branch, so that effect lives in the loop
         beside the turn-budget bookkeeping."
   :when (>= (:consecutive-failures branch) (threshold :stuck-threshold))
   :message-form (str (prompt "stuck")
                      (when-let [c (:last-failed-claim branch)]
                        (str "\n\n**Withheld**, until you put a different approach"
                             " on record:\n\n> " c "\n\nThat claim will not reach an"
                             " engine — any engine — while this stands. Everything"
                             " else is open, including a smaller piece of the same"
                             " goal. The next " (threshold :reframe-grace) " turns are"
                             " yours to change course in; you will not be culled for"
                             " the failures that led here."))
                      ;; The reflexion log: everything this branch has already
                      ;; tried and abandoned (the newest is the withheld claim
                      ;; above), so it diverges instead of circling back to a
                      ;; dead end it has forgotten.
                      (when-let [earlier (seq (butlast (:abandoned branch)))]
                        (str "\n\nApproaches you have already tried and abandoned"
                             " — do not retry these:\n"
                             (str/join "\n" (map #(str "- " %) earlier)))))
   :prediction "the branch retracts, decomposes, or changes technique"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :progress-stalled
   :priority 10
   :budget :max-stall-nudges
   :doc "Turns passing with no progress event, after the branch has shown it
         can make progress. Arms only after the first, so exploration is never
         nudged."
   :when (and (:any-progress? branch)
              (>= (:turns-since-progress branch)
                  (threshold :progress-stall-threshold)))
   :message-form (str (prompt "progress-stalled")
                      "\n\nNothing has advanced in " (:turns-since-progress branch)
                      " turns.")
   :prediction "the branch produces a new confirmed artifact or discharges a sub-claim"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :studying
   :priority 11
   :budget :max-studying-nudges
   :doc "The supervisor's stall: the branch shipped something, then lapsed into
         inspecting — read/grep/eval turn after turn, all succeeding, changing
         nothing. The failure gates key on errors (there are none) and the
         progress gate keys on confirmed artifacts (a coding run makes none),
         so this is the case both are blind to. It watches the branch's own
         turn pattern instead — see samizdat.agent.supervisor, which is also
         what a multi-agent supervisor sub-loop reads. Coding-tunable, since it
         reads tool mechanics, not verification."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (supervisor/over-studying? (tool-vocab :shipping)
                                         (:turns branch)
                                         (threshold :studying-turns)))
   :message-form (supervisor/stall-nudge (tool-vocab :shipping)
                                         (:turns branch)
                                         (threshold :studying-turns))
   :prediction "the branch commits a change, runs a test, or ships"
   :window 3}]

 :forceable-tools
 ;; Schemas for the tools a gate may FORCE via native tool_choice (tier 3b:
 ;; data). Only the terminal tools — forcing a mid-task tool would be the
 ;; harness deciding rather than steering. Minimal: enough for the provider
 ;; to accept the function.
 {"done" {:name "done"
          :description "Finish the task and return the final answer."
          :parameters {:type "object"
                       :properties {:answer {:type "string"
                                             :description "The final answer, or the best partial result so far."}}
                       :required ["answer"]}}
  "give_up" {:name "give_up"
             :description "Abandon the task, stating why it cannot be finished."
             :parameters {:type "object"
                          :properties {:reason {:type "string"}}
                          :required ["reason"]}}}}
